challenge.reasoning:
I tried to break the reviewer's verdict and could not. The doc as written contradicts itself. L338 says "tutte le chiamate avvengono sul thread che ha creato il runtime", L119 says the API "è utilizzabile solo dal thread che ha creato il runtime", and L167 says calls from another thread fail (in debug builds). Against that, L365 says the callback does its work "su un thread dell'applicazione e la risolve con zg_promise_resolve, che il motore esegue sul thread principale". So zg_promise_resolve is meant to be called off the runtime thread, and the engine marshals it back. L232 also says zg_surface_acquire/release are "usabili dal render thread". The doc never lists these exceptions, so the claim that both statements hold together is refuted as written.

The feature itself can be built, and the upstream evidence the reviewer cited is accurate. WTF RunLoop::dispatch is thread-safe: RunLoop.cpp:160-172 takes m_nextIterationLock, appends the function and wakes the loop. The cross-thread note at RunLoop.h:157-163 says starting a timer or calling dispatch from another thread is allowed, while stopping or destroying a timer must happen on its own thread. JSObjectMakeDeferredPromise exists at JSObjectRef.h:490 (implementation at JSObjectRef.cpp:279), and testapi.cpp:452-516 shows how to resolve a promise through the stored resolve/reject functions.

Two small corrections to the reviewer:
(1) The accessor is RunLoop::mainSingleton() (RunLoop.h:100). There is no RunLoop::main(), so the suggested "RunLoop::main().dispatch" will not compile; use RunLoop::mainSingleton().dispatch(...).
(2) Saying "JSC values are not thread-safe" is an oversimplification. The JSC C API does take the VM lock (JSLockHolder, e.g. JSObjectRef.cpp:89/109/121), so a raw JSC context could in principle be touched from another thread while holding the lock. But WebCore's VM is tied to the main thread: CommonVM.cpp:56 has ASSERT(isMainThread()), and WebCore's event loop and microtasks assume the main thread. In ZeroG, calling into a page's JSContext from a worker is therefore unsafe in practice, so the conclusion stands that zg_promise_resolve should not take a JS-backed zg_value*. Also, the example's zg_value_string(call, ...) (L379) ties values to a zg_call that only exists during the callback.

The rest of the reviewer's implication is design advice, not something the source can verify, and it is sound:
- an explicit list of thread-safe entry points;
- passing payloads as copied plain C data;
- keeping resolve/reject alive with JSValueProtect;
- making a resolve a no-op after the view is destroyed or the frame navigates;
- keeping the cheap thread check in release builds.

One thing the doc misses: the wakeup handle (L127, L358) is only correct if a cross-thread dispatch actually signals it. On USE(GENERIC_EVENT_LOOP) and USE(WINDOWS_EVENT_LOOP), RunLoop::setWakeUpCallback (RunLoop.h:147-149) is the hook ZeroG will need for zg_runtime_wakeup_handle, so the host learns when a resolve has been posted.

